<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Xian of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">181–234</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Liu Xie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Xian of Han, Liu Xie, was the last emperor of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, reigning from 189 to 220. A puppet of successive warlords, he came under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> in 196, who ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in his name. In 220 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> forced him to abdicate and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-xian-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Xian of Han" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Xian_Qing_illustration.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Xie was born in 181 to Emperor Ling and his consort Wang Rong. The jealous Empress He poisoned his mother soon after his birth, and he was raised by Emperor Ling's mother, Empress Dowager Dong, under the circumspect title Marquis Dong. His older half-brother Liu Bian, the empress's son, was judged insufficiently solemn, and Emperor Ling hesitated over naming Liu Xie crown prince instead. When Emperor Ling died in 189, Liu Bian was declared emperor, and Liu Xie was made Prince of Bohai and later Prince of Chenliu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 189 the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> as the court fell into chaos, forced Emperor Shao to yield, and enthroned Liu Xie at the age of eight. In 190, when a coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> rose against Dong Zhuo, Dong forced the emperor to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and set fire to Luoyang. After officials led by Wang Yun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> killed Dong in 192, his former subordinates Li Jue and Guo Si took control of the emperor. In 195 he escaped to the ruins of Luoyang, where officials starved.</p><p>In 196 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, shared power with the emperor's generals while in fact commanding, and moved the court to Xu. He issued edicts in the emperor's name and used him to lend legitimacy to his campaigns against rival warlords. In 200 Dong Cheng's plot against Cao Cao, based on a claimed secret edict, was discovered and he was killed; Cao Cao had Dong's pregnant daughter, an imperial consort, executed. In 215 Cao Cao had Empress Fu Shou killed, with her two sons, and made his own daughter Cao Jie empress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> died on 15 March 220, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> soon forced Emperor Xian to abdicate, founding the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>. The former emperor became Duke of Shanyang with a fiefdom of 10 000 households and kept privileges such as not having to kneel when receiving edicts. He died in April 234 and was buried with Han ceremonies fit for an emperor, with the Wei emperor Cao Rui among the mourners. His dukedom lasted 73 more years under three further dukes. Such hereditary nobility for a deposed predecessor was known as two crownings and three respects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Xian of Han” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cao Cao, holding the last Han emperor, <b>Emperor Xian</b>, in his power, reunified northern China after defeating Yuan Shao at Guandu in 200, and became King of Wei in 216.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=215" aria-label="Luoyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7405 909 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Xian of Han"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=215">What was here in 215 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
